INT. ABANDONED CAROUSEL WORKSHOP - NIGHT

A cavernous shed of peeling paint and sawdust. Dozens of wooden horses hang from the rafters on chains, their glass eyes catching the light of a camping lantern. Half-carved zebras lie on workbenches. A pipe organ sits dead under a tarp.

TEODORA VANCE-MBEKI (30s, parka, flashlight clipped to her collar) pushes through the door, brushes cobwebs off her hair, and stops under the hanging horses. DESMOND CHALK (40s, real-estate blazer, laminated clipboard) follows her in, sneezing. Near the back, a gaunt man in a leather apron stands motionless at the workbench with his back to them: OLD HARLAN (70s, hands like roots).

DESMOND
As you can see, the property has character.

TEODORA
It has dust.

DESMOND
Dust is character that has been left alone.

Old Harlan does not turn. Teodora raises her flashlight. The beam finds his shoulder, and then the bench in front of him, where a small wooden horse sits with its mane half-painted in red.

TEODORA
Excuse me, sir? We didn't know anyone was in here.

He lifts the brush. Dips it. Draws a single, slow line down the horse's neck. Desmond steps closer, reading his clipboard as he goes, and trips on a coil of rope. He grabs a hanging horse to steady himself and the chain groans. Teodora catches the clipboard as it flies, and Harlan lays the brush down without a sound.

DESMOND
(whisper)
He wasn't on the listing.

TEODORA
Neither was the smell.

Old Harlan turns. His face is smooth, painted, a carved face with a hinge at the jaw. It opens.

OLD HARLAN
Closing time.

Overhead, every horse begins, very slowly, to turn.
